1.39.m:
Above the sphere of the sages, at just a hundred thousand, stands the Pole Star;
the Pole Star serves as the central pivot of the entire wheel of constellations.
There stands the blessed Dharma, Viṣṇu Nārāyaṇa.

1.44.m:
Eight boundary mountains have been declared here by me, O twice-born;
Jaṭhara and the rest stand in the four directions of Meru, O great ṛṣis.

Thus ends the forty-fourth chapter in the first section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the auspicious Kūrma Purāṇa.

1.45.m:
Sūta said:
In Ketumāla, the men are dark, all feeding on jackfruit;
and the women have the lustre of blue lotus petals, and they live for ten thousand years.

In Ramyaka, men and women delight themselves, radiant as silver;
established in purity, feeding on the fruit of the banyan tree,
they live for ten thousand and one thousand five hundred years.

In Hiraṇmaya, all have a golden hue and feed on breadfruit;
men and women live for eleven thousand and one thousand five hundred years,
just like dwellers in the world of the gods.

1.46.m:
Thus has the extent of Jambūdvīpa been declared in brief;
it could not be described in detail by me even in hundreds of years.

Thus ends the forty-sixth chapter in the former section of the holy Kūrma Purāṇa, in the compilation of six thousand [verses].

1.47.m:
Gomeda is said to be the first of them, and Candra the second;
Nārada, Dundubhi, Soma, and also Ṛṣabha;
Vaibhrāja is declared the seventh, exceedingly dear to Brahmā.

1.48.m:
Maheśvara is beyond the unmanifest; the cosmic egg arises from the unmanifest;
from the cosmic egg Brahmā was born, and by him this world was created.

Thus ends the forty-eighth chapter in the first section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

1.50.m:
In the twenty-seventh, the great sage Jātūkarṇa was Vyāsa;
and when this twenty-eighth Dvāpara age was reached, O twice-born,
the son of Parāśara, Kṛṣṇa Dvaipāyana, became Vyāsa.

He engaged Jaimini as the teacher of the Sāmaveda,
and likewise Sumantu, the best of sages, for the Atharvaveda;
and he appointed me to expound the histories and Purāṇas.

Devoted to the Veda, always [devoted to] the Lord, he knows that which is beyond the Veda to be supreme;
that Lord, Maheśvara, the embodiment of the Veda, is to be known through the Veda;
he alone is the Veda and the object of knowledge; resorting to him alone, one is liberated.

2.3.m:
The Lord said:
From the unmanifest arose time, primordial matter, and the supreme spirit;
from them this entire [universe] was born; therefore the world is permeated by Brahman.

Higher than the breath of life is space; beyond space is the Lord, the fire;
I am he, all-pervading, tranquil, the self of knowledge, the Supreme Lord;
there is no being higher than me; having known me, one is liberated.

In my presence, this time creates the entire world;
the infinite-natured one impels it—this indeed is the teaching of the Veda.

Thus ends the third chapter in the latter section (the Īśvaragītā) in the compilation of six thousand verses of the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.
